PrepFlow
System Architecture Document

Version: v1.0

Status: Draft

Author: Tejas Nere

1. Purpose

This document defines the overall software architecture of PrepFlow.

The architecture is designed to be modular, scalable, maintainable, and future-ready.

Every component should have a clear responsibility and remain loosely coupled.

2. Architecture Overview

PrepFlow follows a modern three-tier architecture.

Student

↓

React Frontend

↓

Node.js + Express API

↓

MongoDB Atlas

↓

Supabase Storage

All communication between frontend and database must happen through backend APIs.

The frontend should never directly access MongoDB.

3. High-Level Architecture
                    Student
                       │
                       ▼
              React Frontend
                       │
                REST API Calls
                       │
                       ▼
            Node.js + Express Backend
               │                │
               │                │
               ▼                ▼
        MongoDB Atlas     Supabase Storage
4. Frontend Responsibilities

The frontend is responsible for:

Rendering UI
API Communication
State Management
Routing
Authentication Handling
Progress Display
User Interaction

The frontend must never contain business logic related to content management.

5. Backend Responsibilities

The backend is responsible for:

Authentication
Authorization
Business Logic
CRUD Operations
File Upload
Validation
Progress Calculation
Analytics Calculation
API Responses
6. Database Responsibilities

MongoDB stores:

Users
Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Progress
Tags
Resource Types

MongoDB never stores actual PDF files.

7. Storage Responsibilities

Supabase Storage stores:

PDFs
Notes
Formula Sheets
Images
Icons

Only file URLs are stored in MongoDB.

8. Data Flow

Student opens chapter

↓

Frontend requests API

↓

Backend validates request

↓

MongoDB returns chapter data

↓

Backend formats response

↓

Frontend renders UI

9. File Upload Flow

Administrator uploads file

↓

Frontend sends file

↓

Backend validates file

↓

Supabase uploads file

↓

Supabase returns URL

↓

MongoDB stores URL

↓

Student accesses file

10. Progress Flow

Student completes resource

↓

Frontend sends completion request

↓

Backend updates Progress collection

↓

Backend recalculates progress

↓

Updated analytics returned

↓

Frontend refreshes UI

11. Authentication Flow

Administrator

↓

Login

↓

JWT Generated

↓

Token Stored

↓

Protected API Access

↓

Logout

Future student authentication should follow the same architecture.

12. Folder Structure Philosophy

The project should follow a feature-based structure.

Frontend and backend should remain independent.

Each module should contain its own logic, routes, controllers, and services.

Avoid placing unrelated code together.

13. Frontend Structure

Recommended modules:

Authentication
Dashboard
Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Analytics
Search
Settings
Admin CMS

Shared components should be reusable across modules.

14. Backend Structure

Recommended modules:

Authentication
Users
Subjects
Chapters
Resources
Progress
Analytics
Storage
Search
Admin

Each module should include:

Routes
Controller
Service
Validation
Model
15. API Communication

Frontend communicates only through REST APIs.

Every request should include proper validation.

Responses should follow a consistent structure.

16. Error Handling

Errors should be handled centrally.

API responses should never expose internal server details.

Meaningful messages should be returned.

17. Validation

Validation should occur in three stages:

Frontend Validation

↓

Backend Validation

↓

Database Validation

No invalid data should reach MongoDB.

18. Scalability

The architecture should support:

Additional Exams
More Subjects
AI Modules
Battle Mode
Mock Tests
Community Features

without major restructuring.

19. Security

Only authenticated administrators can access CMS APIs.

Students cannot access administrative endpoints.

Sensitive configuration should remain inside environment variables.

20. Performance

The application should:

Minimize API calls
Use efficient database queries
Avoid duplicate requests
Load resources lazily where appropriate
21. Reusability

Every component should be reusable.

Business logic should remain inside services.

Controllers should remain lightweight.

UI components should remain generic.

22. Maintainability

Every feature should remain isolated.

Adding new features should not require modifying existing modules unnecessarily.

23. Future Ready Architecture

The architecture should allow easy integration of:

AI Recommendations
Battle Mode
Leaderboards
Notifications
Revision Planner
Community

without redesigning the project.

24. Architectural Principles
Separation of Concerns
Single Responsibility Principle
Reusability
Scalability
Maintainability
Data-Driven Design
Modular Development
25. Golden Rules

Never hardcode content.

Never access MongoDB directly from the frontend.

Never store files inside MongoDB.

Never duplicate business logic.

Always keep modules independent.

Always build for future scalability.

26. Final Statement

PrepFlow's architecture is designed to ensure long-term maintainability and scalability.

The system prioritizes clean separation between presentation, business logic, data management, and content storage.

This architecture allows the platform to grow without requiring major structural changes.